 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T08:45+08:00",
 "answer": "Yes, the haze in the Johor Bahru area is slightly worse than in Singapore, though both regions are experiencing **Unhealthy** air quality levels. \n\nAn overview of the air pollution data from both countries' official monitoring agencies shows:\n\n### **Johor Bahru (Malaysia API)**\nJohor is the primary haze hotspot in Malaysia. Data from the Malaysian Air Pollutant Index Management System (APIMS) indicates the Air Pollution Index (API) readings in and around Johor Bahru are nearing the \"Very Unhealthy\" threshold (which starts at 201):\n* **Pasir Gudang (Eastern JB):** 198 *(highest in Malaysia)*\n* **Larkin (Central JB):** 179\n* **Pengerang & Kota Tinggi (Greater JB area):** 175 and 173\n*(Note: Some independent real-time trackers measuring immediate localized PM2.5 concentrations in parts of JB have even spiked over 200 into the \"Very Unhealthy\" category).*\n\n### **Singapore (Singapore PSI)**\nAccording to Singapore's National Environment Agency (NEA), the haze eased slightly overnight from the previous day's peak (which hit 229), but remains solidly in the \"Unhealthy\" range:\n* **24-Hour PSI:** Ranging from **144 to 191** across the island (with the highest reading of 191 in Central Singapore). \n* **1-Hour PM2.5 Readings:** Ranging from **75 to 119 µg/m³**, which places immediate air quality in the \"Elevated\" band.\n\n**Summary:** \nWhile Central Singapore (191) and Central JB/Larkin (179) are trading closely, eastern Johor Bahru (Pasir Gudang at 198) is experiencing the worst of the pollution in the immediate cross-border region. If you are in either city, health authorities advise reducing prolonged or strenuous outdoor exertion, and vulnerable groups (children, the elderly, and those with respiratory conditions) should stay indoors as much as possible.",
